# PROVENANCE — infinity6e

SigmaStar Infinity6E, the SSC30KQ line. Kernel-side payload. Userspace is
johnchia/sigmastar-lib under the same family name.

Everything prebuilt here was harvested from a shipped camera firmware via
OpenIPC's `sigmastar-osdrv-infinity6e`, **not** taken from a vendor SDK release.
That is now checked rather than assumed -- see "Where these actually came from".

## kmod-4.9.84-0607-glibc-9.1.0/

Prebuilt vendor MI kernel modules, from the same build as the userspace
libraries in johnchia/sigmastar-lib:

    release_0607   built 2022-06-07 09:51-09:52
    project_commit ceb04c4
    sdk_commit     dc99390
    toolchain      GCC 9.1.0, glibc
    vermagic       4.9.84 SMP preempt mod_unload ARMv7 thumb2 p2v8

**The whole flavour is in the directory name because nothing at load time will
catch a mismatch.** The vendor ships these under `<libc>/<gcc>` trees that are
not one source built several ways: across release_0601's own trees, mi_sys.ko
differs by 27-29 imported kernel symbols, differs in `depends=` (mi_common under
glibc/9.1.0, empty under the others), and the module sets differ -- mi_ai.ko,
mi_ao.ko and gc4653_MIPI.ko exist only under glibc/9.1.0. vermagic is byte
identical across all of them and CONFIG_MODVERSIONS is off, so insmod accepts a
foreign module and it fails later at symbol resolution, or misbehaves.

The kernel release leads the name because it is the one component `uname -r`
must agree with, and because it is what makes a wrong-kernel family visible as a
directory rather than as a runtime fault.

The kernel and userspace halves are two parts of one vendor build and must move
together. `soc/sigmastar/infinity6e.mk` holds that drop in one place --
SIGMASTAR_DROP/LIBC/GCC/KREL -- which both packages read, so the two repository
pins cannot drift apart silently.

Do not mix these with modules from another flavour directory.

Previously johnchia/sigmastar-sdk @ cdfe9e3e8421dc6929d1e55dce22ab54e7556fd4,
under infinity6e/kmod/; then 4.9.84/kmod-0607-glibc-9.1.0/.

## sensor-src/

11 drivers. See the top-level PROVENANCE -- source, upstream hash and the reason
for the per-family split are shared with infinity6b0.

## sensor-iq/

Per-sensor API bins -- IQ Tool "save Bin" output, loaded by the application via
MI_ISP_API_CmdLoadBinFile with user_key 1234. Distinct in both format and
purpose from `iqfile/` below; the two are not interchangeable. Naming follows
ingenic-sdk/sensor-iq/<family>/.

## iqfile/

The CUS3A iqfile and its schema. Read from disk by CUS3A itself at AE init, from
whatever MI_ISP_GetIspRoot reports -- /etc/firmware on this board.

Per family, not shared, even though the bytes happen to be identical between
Infinity6E and Infinity6B0: the vendor keeps these per chip
(`project/board/<chip>/iqfile/`, `ispalgo/hal/<family>/inc/iqfile/`) and the i6c
copies differ. The equality across our two families is a fact about these two
chips, not a property of the file, so the path does not claim otherwise.

`iqfile0.bin` is not a chip-generic file. It is the **IMX415 tuning**, renamed:
byte-identical to `imx415_iqfile.bin` in four vendor locations, including both
the infinity6e and infinity6b0 ispalgo trees. It serves as the CUS3A bootstrap
default; the per-sensor tuning the board actually runs on arrives separately
from `sensor-iq/` via the application. Worth knowing before treating it as a
generic default.

## venc_fw/

`chagall.bin` is **VENC firmware**, not ISP and not sensor, despite having lived
under sensor/firmware/ previously. Vendor location:
`project/board/i6e/venc_fw/`, authoritative copy
`sdk/mhal/chip_common/venc/hal/i6e/chagall.bin`.

Per family for the same reason as `iqfile/`: the vendor ships a separate i6b0
copy, and although that copy is byte-identical to i6e's, the i6c ones are not
and differ per release.

## Where these actually came from

Checked against every SigmaStar SDK tree available locally (ssc30kq
release_0601, ssc336q, ssc377 releases 0602/0712/0907) rather than assumed:

    iqfile0.bin   d44143b0  = imx415_iqfile.bin, present in 4 vendor locations
    isp_api.xml   404349e4  matches no vendor copy; the five local ones are
                            4981dbbd 0601/i6e, bb04546f ssc336q/i6e,
                            32e7a63d 0907/i6c, e9c3220a 0602/i6c, 1bd3f6d5 0712/i6c
    chagall.bin   6f109f34  matches no vendor copy; every local vendor i6e and
                            i6b0 copy is 17741e8b

So `isp_api.xml` and `chagall.bin` are from the OpenIPC firmware harvest, the
same origin as the modules, and are *not* the vendor SDK's files despite the
vendor paths named above. Those paths say where the vendor keeps its equivalent,
which is useful for replacing them, and are not a claim about these bytes.

No source is available for the prebuilt artifacts and none is claimed here.

